          <p class="thesis-back fragment">
            All three worked because you <b>prepared</b> them.
          </p>
          <aside class="notes">
            This is the chapter's spine. Everything after it is one of these
            three, or a way of protecting them — say that once, here, and the
            rest of the chapter reads as a list of instances rather than a bag
            of tips.

            Walk them with →, one at a time. Each leg is a TRAILER for one of
            the three live applications you are about to run — do not name
            them here. The demo should land as recognition.

            A YARDSTICK → the requirements audit. The strongest version is not
            "ask for good work" but a written definition of good that it can
            hold work against. Note the direction: this grades what you ALREADY
            have, it is not only a rule for new work. Evidence: giving a model
            an explicit checklist to evaluate against raised agreement with
            human judgement, and letting it revise against that checklist
            improved the result again. If you quote a number, name the
            benchmark — the published gains are on general reasoning tasks,
            not a clean coding experiment. That gap is honest; admit it if
            asked.

            BOTH SIDES → the library migration. This is the leg with hard
            numbers. Across 576,000 generations, roughly a fifth of the
            software packages models recommended did not exist. Put the real
            interfaces in front of it and invented calls on unfamiliar APIs
            drop sharply. The word "correspond" is doing quiet work — it is not
            just two documents, it is how the two map onto each other.
            IMPORTANT CAVEAT, counter-intuitive: this is NOT "always paste in
            the docs." Injecting documentation for things the model already
