            ── HELD ANSWERS ──
            IF CHALLENGED on scripts, the number: SWE-agent, NeurIPS 2024. Same
            model held constant on the same benchmark — a plain shell agent
            scored 11.0%, purpose-built commands 18.0%. Concede freely that it
            measured the harness's tools on 2024 models. SHARPER, and hold it in
            reserve if the room pushes the other way: an iterative search tool
            scored BELOW having no search tool at all. A badly shaped tool is
            worse than no tool, which is an argument for preparing the check
            rather than for buying more tooling.

            IF ASKED "is this safe?" — a script is a smaller thing to allow than
            open bash, and it is where the fuse goes: no production, dry-run
            first. It is not a sandbox. Do not volunteer this; it plants a fear
            the room has not voiced, and the honest version needs the "not a
            sandbox" caveat with it.

            THE VOCABULARY EXAMPLE, verbally and never on the wall: a study of
            models generating code against a real industrial codebase found they
            fall back on common patterns — returning a null pointer where that
            codebase's own rules require an exception. Right shape, wrong house
            style, because nobody ever showed it the house style.

            ── MUST NOT CLAIM ──
            "The agent can only do what you explicitly permitted." FALSE while
            bash is enabled — argument-constraining patterns are fragile, and an
            allowed shell can still reach the network. Scripts are a fuse, not a
            sandbox. The boundary is an OS sandbox plus deny rules.

            "Scripts save tokens because the command is shorter." No. The human's
            typing is a fraction of a percent of the window. The saving is in the
            OUTPUT and in the avoided failure chain — long chains of incorrect
            changes are one of the biggest drivers of token waste.

            98.7%, or 150k → 2k. That is MCP tool-definition loading, a different
            thing entirely. It does not belong to scripts.

            "ASML measured that AI fails." The study used small open models and
            measured whether the code BUILT, not whether it was correct — on
            correctness it scored zero. Quotable only as the mechanism above,
